    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nThis code is expected to transform `{num = 1, str = \"hello\"}` to `{\"num\", 1, \"str\", \"hello\"}`:\n```lua\n_G.func = function(tbl)\n  return vim\n    .iter(tbl)\n    :map(function(k, v)\n      return { k, v }\n    end)\n    :flatten()\n    :totable()\nend\n```\nBut the error is `flatten() requires an array-like table`\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nnvim --clean\n:edit tmp.lua\n*paste example code*\n:luafile %\n:=func({num = 1, str = \"hello\"})\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nTo get `{\"num\", 1, \"str\", \"hello\"}` as the result.\n\nThe workaround is to save temporary result to some variable and apply flatten() to it, and it's pretty simple\n```lua\n_G.func = function(tbl)\n  local tmp = vim\n    .iter(tbl)\n    :map(function(k, v)\n      return { k, v }\n    end)\n    :totable()\n\n  return vim.iter(tmp):flatten():totable()\nend\n```\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nv0.12.4\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nno\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nNixOS 26.11 (Zokor) aarch64\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nkitty 0.48.2\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nnix",
